Source: noust
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Yago López Prado <yago.lopez.adeje@gmail.com>
# Generating the shell completions runs the package, so everything it imports
# at import time is needed at build time too, not only at run time. Leaving
# these out is what made every OBS target fail with ModuleNotFoundError while
# the GitHub runner, which happens to ship python3-yaml, built fine.
# Only what builds a wheel. Nothing here runs the package: the shell
# completions are committed rather than generated, which is what removed
# the whole class of failure where every runtime import became a build
# dependency on every distribution and every architecture.
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13),
               dh-python,
               pybuild-plugin-pyproject,
               python3-all,
               python3-setuptools,
               python3-wheel
Standards-Version: 4.6.0
Homepage: https://github.com/Perkybeet/noust
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: noust
Architecture: all
# Noust was called WASM until 3.0.0. The package that owned /usr/bin/wasm, the
# man page and the completions before then was 'wasm'; it is now a transitional
# package that depends on this one (packaging/transitional/wasm), so an ordinary
# 'apt upgrade' brings noust in. Breaks+Replaces is the Debian way to take over
# another package's files across a rename. The ~ takes in every 2.x and nothing
# of 3.0.0, pre-releases included: a snapshot of the tree before its release
# (packaging/obs/stage.sh --snapshot) is 3.0.0~dev, with a transitional wasm
# of the same version that must be installable alongside it.
Breaks: wasm (<< 3.0.0~)
Replaces: wasm (<< 3.0.0~)
Provides: wasm
Depends: ${python3:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
         python3 (>= 3.10),
         python3-pip,
         python3-venv,
         python3-yaml,
         python3-jinja2,
         python3-click,
         python3-rich,
         nginx | apache2,
         certbot
# python3-venv: noust.deployers.python creates each Python app's virtualenv
# with 'python3 -m venv'. Debian and Ubuntu split venv (and the ensurepip
# bootstrap it runs by default) out of the python3 package; without this,
# every Python deployment fails at 'Creating virtual environment...'.
#
# python3-inquirer does not exist in Debian or Ubuntu, so the interactive mode
# it gated never worked on those distributions. Prompts use questionary, which
# is packaged from Debian 13 and Ubuntu 24.04 onwards.
#
# python3-cryptography verifies passkey signatures (noust.core.accounts.webauthn,
# imported only then). certbot already pulls it in on Debian and Ubuntu.
#
# questionary is a Recommends rather than a Depends because it is absent from
# Debian 12 and Ubuntu 22.04, where a Depends built a package apt then refused
# to install. On those two it is not even recommended (debian/rules decides,
# through ${noust:Recommends}): an unsatisfiable Recommends made `apt upgrade`
# keep wasm back instead of bringing noust in. apt installs Recommends by
# default, so nothing changes on the releases that have it, and on the two that
# do not the package installs and interactive mode explains what is missing.
Recommends: python3-fastapi,
            python3-starlette,
            python3-pydantic,
            python3-uvicorn,
            python3-psutil,
            python3-httpx,
            python3-websockets,
            python3-cryptography,
            ${noust:Recommends}
Suggests: git,
          nodejs,
          npm,
          rclone
Description: deploy and manage web applications on Linux servers
 Noust deploys and manages web applications on the server it runs on. It
 configures Nginx or Apache, obtains and renews TLS certificates, supervises
 systemd services, manages databases and backups, and builds every deploy as
 a health-gated release with instant rollback. An optional browser console
 serves the same operations over a JSON API.
 .
 Noust was called WASM until 3.0.0. The command is noust; wasm remains an
 alias for the whole 3.x series, so existing scripts and cron lines keep
 working.
